    {
      "format": "essay",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "context": "Mei investigates an enzyme that converts a substrate into a product. She measures the initial reaction rate under the conditions shown. All reactions contain the same amount of enzyme. Unless otherwise indicated, reactions are conducted at 37 °C and pH 7 with 1 mM substrate and no inhibitor.\n\n| Condition changed | Initial rate (µmol product/min) |\n|---|---:|\n| Temperature: 10 °C | 12 |\n| None | 40 |\n| Temperature: 70 °C | 2 |\n| pH: 4 | 5 |\n| Inhibitor present | 20 |\n| Substrate: 10 mM | 87 |\n| Substrate: 10 mM; inhibitor present | 71 |\n\nThe inhibitor concentration is the same in both reactions containing the inhibitor.",
      "question": "(a) Describe the effect of temperature on the reaction rate across the temperatures tested. Explain why the rate at 10 °C is lower than the rate at 37 °C.\n\n(b) Explain how the change from pH 7 to pH 4 could reduce the enzyme's activity.\n\n(c) The inhibitor is proposed to compete with the substrate for the enzyme's active site. Explain how the data are consistent with this proposal. Predict the effect of increasing the substrate concentration well beyond 10 mM on the difference between the rates with and without the inhibitor.\n\n(d) Design a follow-up experiment to test whether exposure to 70 °C causes a persistent loss of enzyme activity after the enzyme is returned to 37 °C. Include an appropriate control and predict the results if the heat exposure causes a persistent loss of activity.",
      "rubric": [
        {
          "criterion": "(a) Temperature trend",
          "description": "Earn 1 point for describing that the rate is highest at 37 °C and lower at both 10 °C and 70 °C.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "(a) Low-temperature explanation",
          "description": "Earn 1 point for explaining that lower temperature reduces molecular motion and the frequency of effective enzyme-substrate collisions.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "(b) Effect of pH",
          "description": "Earn 1 point for explaining that a change in pH can alter the charges of amino acid side chains, changing interactions that maintain the enzyme's shape or active site and reducing activity.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "(c) Evidence for competition",
          "description": "Earn 1 point for explaining that the inhibitor produces a smaller proportional reduction in rate at 10 mM substrate than at 1 mM substrate, consistent with substrate competing with the inhibitor.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "(c) Prediction",
          "description": "Earn 1 point for predicting that, at a much higher substrate concentration, the rates with and without inhibitor will become more similar.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "(d) Experimental treatment",
          "description": "Earn 1 point for proposing that enzyme be exposed to 70 °C, returned to 37 °C, and then assayed for activity under the same conditions as a control.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "(d) Control",
          "description": "Earn 1 point for including a control enzyme sample held at 37 °C for the same exposure period before its activity is measured.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "(d) Predicted results",
          "description": "Earn 1 point for predicting that the heat-exposed sample will have a lower rate than the control after both are returned to or maintained at 37 °C.",
          "points": 1
        }
      ],
      "keyPoints": [
        "The observed rate is highest at 37 °C. Low temperature slows molecular motion, while high temperature can disrupt interactions that maintain enzyme structure.",
        "A pH change can alter amino acid charges and the shape or chemical properties of the active site.",
        "Increasing substrate concentration reduces the inhibitor's proportional effect, consistent with competitive inhibition, although the data alone do not prove the mechanism.",
        "A controlled heat-exposure experiment can distinguish a persistent loss of activity from an effect that occurs only during measurement at 70 °C."
      ],
      "sampleOutline": "(a) The rate rises from 10 °C to 37 °C and falls at 70 °C. At 10 °C, fewer effective collisions occur per unit time. (b) At pH 4, altered side-chain charges can disrupt interactions that maintain the active site. (c) The inhibitor reduces the rate by half at 1 mM substrate but by a smaller fraction at 10 mM, consistent with competition. At a much higher substrate concentration, the two rates should become more similar. (d) Hold one enzyme sample at 70 °C and a control at 37 °C for the same time. Return the heated sample to 37 °C and assay both under identical conditions. A persistently lower rate in the heated sample would support a lasting loss of activity."
    }
  ]
}

